    <body>
      <sec id="article-78063.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) is a clinicoradiographic neurovascular disorder characterized by acute or subacute onset of seizures, encephalopathy, headache, and visual disturbances, often occurring in the setting of uncontrolled or rapidly rising blood pressure. Although classically associated with posterior cerebral white matter edema on imaging, PRES can involve multiple brain regions and is not always reversible. Prompt recognition is essential, as delayed diagnosis and treatment may result in permanent neurologic deficits, epilepsy, or life-threatening complications. Risk factors include hypertension, renal disease, autoimmune disorders, preeclampsia, sepsis, and exposure to cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapies.</p>
        <p>This course reviews the clinical features, diagnostic studies, and management of PRES across diverse clinical settings. Participants will also gain an understanding of key clinical predictors, the interpretation of neuroimaging findings, the distinction between PRES and stroke and other mimics, and the implementation of safe blood pressure and seizure management strategies.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying PRES early in its clinical course, etiologic assessment, performing the recommended evaluation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition&#x000a0;to reduce morbidity and improve neurologic outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical features of posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome that should facilitate prompt evaluation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome from other neurologic conditions using diagnostic findings.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate underlying etiologies of posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome to guide targeted treatment and prevent recurrence.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate management strategies with interprofessional team members to improve care coordination and improve outcomes for patients with posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=78063&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=78063">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) is a clinicoradiographically diagnosed neurovascular disorder, also known as reversible posterior leukoencephalopathy syndrome (RPLS) and hyperperfusion encephalopathy. Individuals with PRES can present with altered mentation or stupor, drowsiness, visual disturbances, eg, visual hallucinations, cortical blindness, hemianopia, quadrantanopia, and diplopia, seizures that may be focal or generalized tonic-clonic, and headaches.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r1">[1]</xref> PRES can develop acutely or subacutely, with symptoms emerging within hours to days. Presentations often occur in the setting of acute uncontrolled hypertension, with systolic blood pressures of 160 mm Hg and above, commonly ranging from 160 to 190 mm Hg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>The designation of PRES reflects both its clinical and radiographic features. Radiographically, MRI commonly reveals white matter edema, appearing as hyperintense T2 signals or hypointense T1 signals, most frequently in the posterior cerebrum symmetrically, though asymmetric patterns can occur. The term PRES also emphasizes the potential reversibility of symptoms when recognition and prompt treatment occur. However, the syndrome&#x02019;s name can be misleading, as edema does not always localize to the posterior cerebral white matter. Edema can extend beyond the parietal-occipital regions into watershed zones, involve the thalamus, and occasionally affect the anterior circulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r6">[6]</xref> PRES does not always resolve, and some patients may experience life-threatening complications, eg, transforaminal cerebellar herniation or focal neurologic deficits, particularly if treatment is delayed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Individuals at risk for developing PRES often have&#x000a0;1 or more contributing factors, triggers, or underlying etiologies. Common risk factors include hypertension, rapid rises in blood pressure, preeclampsia, kidney disease such as nephrotic syndrome&#x02014;which can cause hypovolemia and secondary hypertension through activation of the renin-angiotensin system&#x02014;renal failure, and liver disease. Exposure to certain chemotherapy agents, including platinum-containing medications, gemcitabine, and CHOP/R-CHOP regimens (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, hydroxydaunorubicin [doxorubicin], oncovin [vincristine], and prednisone), as well as cytotoxic medications or immunosuppressants, eg, tacrolimus, sirolimus, interferon therapies, and various immunotherapies or monoclonal antibodies, eg, bevacizumab, pazopanib, sorafenib, and sunitinib, also increases risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Autoimmune disorders, including hemolytic uremic syndrome, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, and systemic lupus erythematosus, as well as sepsis or allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, further contribute to PRES susceptibility.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r15">[15]</xref> Among these triggers and etiologies, uncontrolled hypertension occurs most frequently; however, acute hypertension alone does not predict PRES development, and identifying which hypertensive individuals will progress to PRES remains challenging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>PRES is a historically underdiagnosed syndrome, perhaps due to limited awareness; however, with the increasing availability of imaging and improvements in its quality, awareness of PRES has increased, and an understanding of its epidemiology has emerged. Based on several reports and studies, PRES affects people of all ages but&#x000a0;is more common in middle-aged women.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>In a retrospective study involving the review of demographics, risk factors, and clinical presentation of 113 people with PRES, Fugate and colleagues found that 51 individuals (45%) had an autoimmune disease with most of the individuals&#x000a0;presenting&#x000a0;with seizures (74%), followed by encephalopathy (28%), headache (26%), and visual disturbance (20%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r4">[4]</xref> Having an epileptic seizure at presentation was found to be the best predictor for PRES, and encephalopathy was the second-best predictor.&#x000a0;The same study found that having a recent&#x000a0;exposure to chemotherapy and those with underlying renal failure were also crucial predictors of PRES.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathophysiology of PRES remains incompletely understood, but dysregulation of cerebral autoregulation has been proposed as a central mechanism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref> Cerebral autoregulation maintains constant cerebral blood flow across a range of blood pressures by adjusting vascular resistance. When blood pressure rises, typically above a systolic pressure of 160 mm Hg in most individuals, maximal vasoconstriction occurs, and cerebral blood flow begins to increase with further pressure elevation. Elevated hydrostatic pressure can disrupt the blood-brain barrier, allowing intravascular fluid to leak into brain tissue and produce vasogenic edema. The posterior circulation appears particularly susceptible, likely due to fewer adaptive mechanisms that regulate fluid extravasation and barrier integrity under high blood pressure compared with the anterior circulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r19">[19]</xref> Findings from a formaldehyde histofluorescence study by Edvinsson and colleagues demonstrated a greater density of adrenergic perivascular fibers in the anterior circulation, suggesting that enhanced sympathetic innervation supports more robust autoregulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Critiques of the hypertensive-hyperperfusion hypothesis note that some individuals with PRES present with blood pressures below the brain's autoregulatory threshold.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r21">[21]</xref> Not all cases involve hypertension. Case reports document PRES in normotensive patients receiving cytotoxic therapies, eg, tacrolimus after liver transplantation, supporting the role of endothelial dysfunction in disease development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r22">[22]</xref> Tacrolimus, cyclosporine, cisplatin, and other immunosuppressants can directly damage the endothelium, compromising the blood-brain barrier.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Endothelial compromise also arises from neuroinflammation, as seen in sepsis, metabolic disorders, eg, uremia and hypomagnesemia, and autoimmune diseases. Astrogliosis, microgliosis, and endothelial activation in sepsis, along with endothelial injury in ANCA-associated vasculitides, increase blood-brain barrier permeability, promoting extravasation of intravascular fluid and facilitating PRES.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Ischemia has also been proposed as a contributing mechanism. Dysregulated autoregulation could lead to vasoconstriction and cytotoxic edema, with some radionuclide studies showing vasoconstriction in affected patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r29">[29]</xref> However, these findings reflect a minority of cases, and subsequent pathology studies have largely failed to demonstrate ischemia or infarction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r30">[30]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>PRES requires a clinical and radiographic diagnosis, making a thorough history and physical examination essential. Clinical presentations vary in both severity and acuity, with the most common symptoms including seizures in 80% of patients, headache in 50%, visual disturbances in 39%&#x02014;eg, binocular diplopia, vision loss, no light perception, hemianopia, or quadrantanopia&#x02014;encephalopathy in 28%, and focal neurological deficits in 10% to 15%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r31">[31]</xref> Patients may present with altered mental status, limiting the reliability of their history, which makes obtaining collateral information from relatives, friends, or caregivers especially valuable.</p>
        <p>Most patients present with moderately to severely elevated blood pressure, with up to 75% affected, although a small subset may remain normotensive.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r21">[21]</xref> Clinicians should pay particular attention to individuals experiencing rapid increases in blood pressure, as this can trigger PRES.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r11">[11]</xref> Physical examination should include assessment for hemianopia, quadrantanopia, visual neglect, cortical blindness, horizontal gaze palsy with intact vestibulo-ocular reflex, papilledema, oral trauma (eg, tongue biting during seizures), brisk reflexes, active convulsions, and urinary or fecal incontinence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r33">[33]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Imaging remains essential and serves as the gold standard for diagnosing PRES, since clinical symptoms alone cannot establish a definitive diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r34">[34]</xref> A head computed tomography (CT) scan plays a critical role in evaluating neurologic emergencies that can present with acute-onset altered mentation, headache, and seizures, eg, intracranial hemorrhage. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain without intravenous contrast represents the imaging modality of choice, allowing identification of vasogenic edema, which appears as a hyperintense signal on T2-weighted fluid-attenuated inversion recovery (FLAIR) sequences, thereby improving diagnostic sensitivity. Edema most commonly involves the parieto-occipital lobes but can extend to the temporal lobe, the frontal lobe (superior frontal gyrus), the cerebellum, the brainstem, and the deep white matter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r6">[6]</xref> The cortex is often more spared than the white matter due to differences in blood flow regulation, although cortical involvement can occur.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;MRI also aids in evaluating alternative diagnoses, eg, hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, posterior circulation infarct, and primary central nervous system vasculitis. In cases involving the classic posterior cerebral hemispheres, sparing of the calcarine and paramedian occipital regions distinguishes PRES from posterior cerebral artery infarct. Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) further differentiates PRES from acute stroke, appearing hypo- or isointense on B1000 sequences.&#x000a0;Importantly, restricted diffusion in MRI can occur in a subset of PRES and may reflect cytotoxic edema and ischemic injury rather than purely vasogenic edema. The presence of cytotoxic edema or diffusion restriction has been associated with worse functional outcomes and incomplete reversibility in some cohorts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;PRES may also be complicated by intracranial hemorrhage (eg, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, subarachnoid hemorrhage, or microhemorrhages). Hemorrhagic PRES is reported in approximately 17% of cases in pooled analyses and is often associated with poorer outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Vascular imaging with either CT angiography (CTA) or magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) of the brain typically appears normal in PRES but can occasionally reveal focal vasoconstriction or vasodilation patterns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Because PRES and reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS) can overlap clinically and radiographically, angiographic vasoconstriction should also raise the possibility of coexisting RCVS rather than vasculitis alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Magnetic resonance venography (MRV) also generally appears normal and can help exclude sagittal sinus thrombosis as a potential diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, the evaluation of PRES includes evaluating potential etiologies, which proves crucial for effective management. Laboratory studies can identify imbalances, eg, uremia, hypomagnesemia, hypoalbuminemia, protein deficiency, and hypoglycemia. Lumbar puncture is sometimes performed in immunocompromised patients or when infection is suspected, providing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) studies that can detect underlying infections, including herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2, as well as autoimmune processes.</p>
        <p>Electroencephalography (EEG) can assist in patients with PRES, particularly when altered mentation persists without overt tonic, clonic, or tonic-clonic movements, suggesting subclinical seizure activity. EEG patterns, however, generally remain nonspecific for diagnosing PRES.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r41">[41]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of PRES requires careful treatment of hypertension along with identification, treatment, and control of the underlying etiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r5">[5]</xref> No specific antihypertensive regimen has been established for acute hypertension associated with PRES.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r42">[42]</xref> Treatment is recommended when blood pressure exceeds 160/110 mm Hg, with target levels of 130 to 150 mm Hg systolic and 80 to 100 mm Hg diastolic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r33">[33]</xref> Rapid or excessive reduction in blood pressure increases the risk of cerebral hypoperfusion and ischemia; therefore, the reduction should not exceed 25% of the presenting blood pressure during the first few hours.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r33">[33]</xref> Such precise acute management may require admission to an intensive care unit for titratable intravenous antihypertensives, including nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol. Transition to oral or nondrip intravenous agents typically follows, with continued blood pressure control on medical floors and in outpatient settings. The duration of postacute antihypertensive therapy remains variable among individuals.</p>
        <p>Some patients with PRES develop life-threatening complications, eg, status epilepticus or coma, warranting management in an intensive care unit.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r43">[43]</xref> No established antiepileptic regimen exists for seizures associated with PRES, and studies defining optimal therapy remain limited; however, levetiracetam and phenytoin commonly serve as treatment options.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r42">[42]</xref> Antiepileptic therapy may begin during the acute phase and be discontinued after resolution of PRES.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;In select cases, epilepsy develops and requires long-term antiepileptic treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Clinical practice lacks scientific evidence supporting steroid use for PRES management, despite their theoretical benefit in reducing vasogenic edema. Steroids have also served as triggers for PRES in some individuals receiving treatment for asthma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r45">[45]</xref> When PRES associates with immunosuppressive agents, eg, bevacizumab, pazopanib, sorafenib, or sunitinib, dose reduction or substitution of the offending medication remains recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r46">[46]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnoses of PRES are broad, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Intracranial hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subdural hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subarachnoid hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cerebral sinus venous thrombosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Thrombosis of the basilar artery</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Encephalitis (autoimmune or infectious etiologies like HSV)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uremic encephalopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypoglycemia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>If recognized and treated early, PRES has a favorable prognosis, with symptoms improving within days, weeks, or months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Visual symptoms often resolve completely, especially with early treatment of PRES; however, some cases have reported residual visual deficits (albeit improved) at 3 to 4 months after onset.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;Although rare, which individuals are at risk of having prolonged visual deficits and persistent seizures is unclear. PRES symptom irreversibility can ensue if treatment is delayed or in cases of significant cerebral edema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>If the amount of cerebral vasogenic edema is significant, the prognosis can worsen, as the increased pressure on the surrounding blood vessels can compromise blood flow and result in ischemia, stroke, malignant intracranial hypertension, or herniation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;In certain situations, prognosis can worsen with involvement of the brainstem and result in death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Recurrence of PRES is possible and has been reported in individuals taking chemotherapy or undergoing dialysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r49">[49]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>PRES can lead to significant complications, particularly when diagnosis or treatment faces a delay. Although symptoms often improve with timely management, some patients develop permanent neurologic sequelae, including focal deficits, persistent cognitive impairment, or chronic epilepsy. Severe complications include status epilepticus, coma, and ischemic injury resulting from cerebral hypoperfusion or cytotoxic edema. Transforaminal cerebellar herniation represents a life-threatening complication reported in severe cases, especially among pediatric patients following hematopoietic stem cell transplantation.</p>
        <p>Visual deficits may persist due to cortical involvement, and recurrent PRES episodes can occur when underlying triggers, eg, uncontrolled hypertension, renal dysfunction, autoimmune disease, or continued exposure to offending medications, remain unaddressed. These complications underscore the importance of early recognition, careful blood pressure management, identification of underlying etiologies, and close neurologic monitoring to reduce morbidity and mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-78063.r50">[50]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence of PRES focuses on early identification and modification of risk factors, particularly strict control of blood pressure and careful monitoring of patients at increased risk. Individuals with chronic hypertension, renal disease, autoimmune conditions, preeclampsia, or exposure to cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapies benefit from regular blood pressure monitoring and timely adjustment of medications to avoid rapid elevations. Clinicians should remain vigilant when initiating or escalating therapies, eg, tacrolimus, chemotherapy agents, or monoclonal antibodies, as early neurologic symptoms may signal the development of PRES. Prompt evaluation of headaches, visual changes, seizures, or altered mental status with appropriate imaging supports early intervention and reduces the likelihood of severe neurologic complications.</p>
        <p>Patient education plays a critical role in prevention and early detection. Patients and caregivers should receive clear counseling on recognizing warning signs, eg, new-onset seizures, vision changes, confusion, severe headaches, or sudden blood pressure elevations, and should understand the importance of seeking urgent medical attention if these symptoms occur. Education should also emphasize medication adherence, home blood pressure monitoring when appropriate, and regular follow-up for underlying conditions, eg, hypertension or renal disease. Empowering patients to understand risk factors and early symptoms supports timely care, reduces recurrence, and improves long-term neurologic outcomes.&#x000a0;For instance, educating patients about blood pressure management can decrease the likelihood of cerebral autoregulation dysfunction, leading to PRES and other complications. Knowledge of the effects of cytotoxic immunosuppressants on the blood-brain barrier, even in people without hypertension, can help clinicians be more mindful of the risk of PRES in individuals taking these medicines.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-78063.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>PRES is a clinicoradiographic neurovascular disorder characterized by acute or subacute onset of seizures, encephalopathy, headache, and visual disturbances, most often occurring in the setting of uncontrolled or rapidly rising blood pressure. MRI findings of vasogenic edema, classically involving the parieto-occipital regions but frequently extending beyond posterior circulation territories, support the diagnosis. Although symptoms may resolve with timely treatment, PRES does not guarantee reversibility, and delayed recognition can lead to permanent neurologic deficits, epilepsy, or life-threatening complications. Common risk factors include hypertension, renal disease, autoimmune disorders, sepsis, preeclampsia, and exposure to cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapies, highlighting the need for heightened clinical awareness.</p>
        <p>Optimal management of PRES relies on coordinated interprofessional care. Physicians, general practitioners, and advanced practitioners require strong diagnostic skills to recognize early clinical patterns, interpret neuroimaging, identify precipitating etiologies, and implement careful blood pressure and seizure management strategies. Nurses play a critical role in continuous neurologic assessment, blood pressure monitoring, seizure observation, and patient education. Pharmacists contribute by optimizing antihypertensive and antiepileptic regimens, monitoring for drug-related triggers, and advising on medication adjustments. Effective interprofessional communication ensures timely escalation of care, safe transitions between inpatient and outpatient settings, and consistent patient education. Collaborative care coordination enhances patient safety, reduces complications, and supports improved neurologic outcomes through shared decision-making and vigilant follow-up.</p>
      </sec>
